The separate live macOS feature trial completed with verified code and functional review on the same frozen commit, native test receipts, no workflow validation skips and clean cleanup. See [live trial](live-opencode-trial.md) and [machine-readable evidence](live-opencode-evidence.json). Earlier fixture counts and provider skips above describe their specific runs.
# Installation and operational troubleshooting

Use the exact dependency versions listed in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md) and Bun from the package manifest (`.bun-version` in a source checkout). Run the package probe from the source checkout: `bun run test:package`.

| Symptom | Check or action |
| --- | --- |
| Tarball install mentions a developer's home directory | Rebuild the current manifest; it no longer contains a self-dependency. Copy the archive into the consumer and install `./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz`. Avoid percent-encoded absolute tarball URLs with Bun 1.4.3. |
| Plugin fails to load on a path with spaces or Windows | Generate the canonical package-directory URL with `realpathSync` and `pathToFileURL` using the README command. Use the encoded URL for plugin loading; do not hand-build drive-letter file URLs. |
| Slash command missing | Restart the pinned OpenCode server in the correct repository and inspect `plugins` in its local `opencode.json`. V2 activates external plugins asynchronously; allow activation to finish. All seven Ralph commands are registered. Setup/orchestrator ask questions and approvals in the current chat. |
| Wrapper prints the OpenCode version or exits before result collection | Rebuild this version and keep standalone Bun on PATH. Wrappers must launch Bun rather than the compiled host executable. |
| Proposal rejects an empty unsupportedPlatforms map | Rebuild this version; the platform-exclusion map is partial. |
| Global OpenCode behaves differently | Use OpenCode 2.0.29. This package uses the v2 plugin contract; v1 is no longer supported. |
| Local test CLI says postinstall was not run | Run `bun pm trust @opencode/cli`; the manifest trusts only this pinned CLI install script. |
| HTTP SDK returns 401 | Pass the v2 server Basic-auth header to `createAdapterClient(url, fetch, headers)`. The smoke test uses an isolated test-only password. |
| Dashboard inaccessible | Get a fresh URL from `/ralph-status`. Use its loopback host and fragment token. Tokens are process-scoped and change on server restart. The listener stops when the plugin is disposed. |
| Dashboard returns 401/403/405 | Authentication is required for API reads. Cross-origin and mutation requests are rejected. Use OpenCode for controls. |
| Reopened workflow does not continue | Recovery intentionally waits for `/ralph-resume`. Read the recovery facts first; resolve blocked/conflicting Git, session or ownership evidence. Attempts and revision budgets do not reset. |
| Opening an executor worktree cancels its parent run | Use this version: repository host ownership is shared through the Git common directory across isolated plugin locations. |
| Another live OpenCode host owns this repository | Close that repository in the other host before activating Ralph here. A live owner is never stolen; stale birth identities recover on the next activation. |
| Writer lock or process ownership cannot be inspected | Do not delete state or kill arbitrary PIDs. Recovery only reclaims provably dead local owners. Native Windows requires PowerShell/CIM and `taskkill.exe`; POSIX requires `ps` and process-group signals. Unknown ownership is a visible blocker. Network-filesystem lock atomicity is not promised. |
| Completion says incomplete/unverified | Read skipped checks, unavailable criteria/review, failed cleanup and retained resources in the report. A successful assistant response does not validate checks. |
| Failed worktree remains | Keep it with its evidence for diagnosis. Do not remove a worktree referenced by an active operation. Successful issue cleanup is owned by integration, not package installation. |
| Live smoke is skipped | The credentialed probe requires opt-in, endpoint, model and v2 server authentication documented in README. A deterministic local model or fake adapter cannot prove provider compatibility. |
| Linux or Windows result is unavailable | Run the corresponding native CI matrix job. Missing or failed supported-platform evidence remains a release blocker. macOS results cannot certify Windows cleanup behavior. |

No install or release script pushes, publishes, creates a PR or merges a feature into its target. The two-repository demonstration uses local temporary Git branches only. Runtime state is under the Git common directory; logs, repositories, test fixtures and secrets are excluded from the package's allowlisted contents. The build removes stale `dist` output before compilation.

### A Ralph question appears without a terminal answer control

All Ralph questions and approvals use normal replies in the current chat. The workflow tool returns when waiting for you, and your next message resumes that exact request. `/ralph-answer` is removed. If you resumed in a different chat, answer the refreshed question there; old request identities are rejected. Workers and reviewers still execute in their own sessions/worktrees, with progress and results shown in the main chat.

### Review cannot verify historical TDD

New executor jobs retain native test-tool receipts for final review. A terminal `unverified` report may be retried explicitly with `/ralph-resume <run-id> --review` when the feature commit and approved commands are unchanged and cleanup completed. This consumes another review round and preserves the original report in the journal. Missing history remains missing evidence, never an inferred pass.

### Correct directory, but Ralph commands are still missing

Restarting the OpenCode terminal UI can reconnect to the same long-running background service. After changing a local plugin path, that service can report the new configuration while retaining an active plugin from the old path and exposing no Ralph commands. Compare the authenticated native `plugin.list({location})` source with the configured package path and inspect `command.list({location})` for the exact repository.

Refreshing the idle repository location with the authenticated SDK’s `client.debug.location.evict({location: {directory}})` forces that location to load the current package. Wait for its active plugin and command inventory afterward. This refreshes the selected repository rather than terminating the shared service and other projects. A live reproduction on the ordinary user service loaded the old `node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin` copy despite the new global config; after refresh, all six commands appeared in the API and in plain `opencode` slash autocomplete.

### Discovery reports a missing guessed search directory

Do not create `src/project` to satisfy a discovery guess. Current command discovery tolerates missing read-only search paths and can continue to a valid proposal. Permission denials and other tool failures still block. The current assistant can correct an exploratory search and continue. `/ralph-commands` uses normal chat approval and the validated save tool; it no longer depends on a separate discovery session.

### Remove Ralph from a project
Run `/ralph-remove`, review the exact deletion preview, and approve in chat. It removes `.ralph` and saved workflow state for the repository, preserving code, Git history, feature branches and workspaces. Linked worktrees share that saved state. Active workers or unsettled cleanup block deletion; use `/ralph-stop` and resolve the listed facts before requesting a new preview. The globally installed plugin and active host bookkeeping are retained.
# Workflow controls and recovery

## Summary
OpenCode registers pause/resume/stop commands and invokes reconciliation on restart.
Recovery observes actual state, reports facts, and waits for explicit resume. Native orchestrator/resume work runs through a visible chat tool; workers and reviewers keep their independent sessions and worktrees.

## Key behavior
- Native `/ralph-orchestrator` offers correlated existing-run choices through the native question adapter. A run without an accepted PRD offers fresh discovery or retention; accepted planning offers saved-stage resume, retention, and restart while execution has not begun. Unsupported protocols require a fresh workflow and are not migrated.
- Pre-PRD recovery cancels and inspects unfinished interview/PRD jobs; it records settled unfinished work rather than requiring completion evidence for a draft that will not be accepted. Unconfirmed cancellation or busy/unknown sessions retain the blocker.
- Discovery restart stops and inspects the recorded stage sessions before releasing the exact repository/run owner through a durable release intent/result. Cleanup uncertainty, foreign locks, pending operations and execution work block release. History, drafts and worktrees remain; released runs cannot resume. A fresh interview starts with its opening question and empty transcript.
- Native `/ralph-resume` continues saved stage ownership directly. Reactivation precedes guarded planning writes; it does not rebind a native owner into the invoking chat. Accepted PRD/graph intents finish immutable publication and the necessary handoff or execution boundary. Execution continuation occurs once when acceptance finalization already started it.
- Reconciliation verifies accepted PRD bytes and scope authorization, repository ownership and stable host prompt receipts. A supported live authoring attempt with correlated delivery retains its owner and remaining budget through recovery freeze; stopped/blocked owners stay retired. Unknown delivery evidence blocks repeated dispatch. Recovery remains observational until explicit resume.
- Native PRD and issue authoring each allow three total attempts per approved stage, independent of issue/review budgets. Only accepted completion tools advance the stage. A settled turn with final prose, failed turn, or active-time timeout consumes an attempt; correctable completion validation stays in the live attempt.
- Authoring attempts persist the owning session, prompt identity, draft root, captured remaining active-time budget and terminal result. Pending product/approval questions, direct native question tools and pause suspend the deadline. The injected planning clock supports deterministic deadline checks.
- Failure retires tool authority before cancelling the recorded planning job. Retry requires confirmed cancellation and an idle/missing inspected owner; missing PRD interview sessions require intervention. PRD retries retain the same interview session; issue retries use reconciled fresh sessions. Each retry writes a separate draft namespace and receives accepted inputs, decision history, prior draft paths and the failure reason. Retired sessions retain their planning tool restrictions.
- Exhaustion retains the blocker and native recovery request. Only a correlated real native “Reset budget and retry” answer grants another three attempts, with answer provenance recorded in `budgetResets`; “Stop” stops the run. Unavailable native input leaves the request pending and publishes its diagnostic. Stop/pause racing cancellation cannot allocate another attempt. Accepted completion atomically terminates the planning job, so its later turn settlement cannot cancel execution or consume another attempt. Authorized scope transitions retire superseded authoring jobs without aborting the continuing session; obsolete jobs cannot block final review.
- Historical chat API only: `/ralph-orchestrator` and `/ralph-resume` prompted the main assistant to use `ralph_workflow`. The awaited tool shows worker roles, attempts, selected commands, results and final review reports. No detached top-level workflow runs behind a slash-command acknowledgement.
- Historical chat API: a durable question ends the visible step with `waiting_for_reply`. The next real user prompt is captured by OpenCode's prompt hook and bound to requests that already existed; `reply` accepts neither model-supplied answer text nor an approval without a new user message. One message settles at most one request. Replies during an unfinished step do not approve a newly generated proposal.
- Historical chat API: ordinary affirmative replies normalize to the current approval revision. Requested changes re-enter planning and require fresh approval. `/ralph-answer` and its custom TUI dialogs are removed. The TUI only shows scoped status notifications.
- Historical chat API: explicit resume can move an idle feature to the current chat, refreshing unanswered request identities. Active workers prevent concurrent resume; old-session replies cannot answer the new requests. Worker sessions cannot call administrative workflow, removal, setup or control tools. Historical command-verification receipts attached to the main session are excluded from worker classification.
- Interrupting the visible tool stops its owned run, workers and commands using existing cleanup. Progress/result output is bounded and redacts runtime secrets; pending questions and complete worker identities precede logs in the result.
- `/ralph-remove` previews deletion of the checkout's `.ralph` and repository-shared saved workflow state. A new user approval is required. It checks the unchanged preview under setup/write locks and refuses active/unsettled jobs or uncertain live processes. It preserves code, branches and workspaces, including unregistered retained workspace directories. Linked-worktree scope is disclosed. Live host bookkeeping remains while the plugin is loaded, allowing a later setup in the same session.
- Native resume drives the saved phase; it does more than flip the control flag. Pending planning, failed baseline retry, scheduling, and automatic review continuation retain approvals and attempt budgets.
- Historical chat API: before any issue attempts, an explicit `--base` selection after failed baseline cleanup invalidates execution approval and presents the new SHA through the normal chat approval gate. It cannot reset attempts or replace an accepted baseline.
- Pause rejects new jobs and suspends review scheduling while current jobs settle. Production executor submissions retain their attempt identity; mergers and successful in-flight checks wait at the next scheduling/ref boundary rather than becoming failed retries. Each resumed in-flight boundary rechecks the original epoch so stop followed by resume cannot revive a stale callback.
- Stop cancels bound sessions, owned processes and registered environments with bounded cleanup. Late job results cannot advance stopped runs.
- Process ownership requires PID birth identity, owner token, repository and workspace. Owned wrappers watch parent liveness; the dashboard stays in the plugin process.
- Recovery shares the Git common-directory store across worktrees. Already advanced integration intents require exact SHA/check revision evidence and record completion once. Ambiguous operations block resume; validation without output never passes.
- Stop retains existing recovery/cleanup blockers, including repeated stops. Resume cannot race pending stop cleanup. Reconciliation must resolve retained ambiguity.
- Recovery binds production validation to the exact merger envelope, candidate, command hash/revision and approval. It restores the accepted issue result even if the ref or operation result was written before a crash. A proven old index/clean worktree is synchronized with a safe two-tree merge; local changes block recovery.
- Review counters, attempt identities, approvals and artifact revisions are retained; controls do not reset budgets. Historical chat API resume renews and republishes unanswered main-session questions while preserving recorded answers and remaining active-time budgets.
- Recovery holds a pending control marker through inspection and checks its epoch/revision before publishing. It verifies logical active-run lock identity and the latest accepted refs; uncertain execution/merge/repair completion blocks instead of repeating dispatch.
- An executing attempt with no submission/candidate can be recovered as a consumed failure only after a matching cancelled job, completed abort, idle/missing exact session and proven environment cleanup. Work and attempt history remain available to the next explicit retry. Busy, unknown or mismatched sessions stay blocked.
- Journal locks are replaced only after inspecting their recorded owner birth identity. Missing or corrupt owner evidence does not authorize replacement.

